mapped debug registers have their effects on completion of the read or write ope\ration.)/IT/Replace/M(D:20090310123149Z)/P 12540 0 R/T(ARM_2009_Q1)/Subj(Replacement Text)/RC(<?xml version="1.0"?><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:8.0.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2" ><p dir="ltr"><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:9.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-wei\ght:normal;font-style:normal">&#8226; </span><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:10.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal">Any completed access to a memory-mapped debug registers is guaranteed to affect the generation of Soft\ware debug events and OS Unlock Catch debug events by subsequent instructions only after one of:&#13;</span><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:9.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal">    &#8212; </span><span dir="ltr" style\="font-size:10.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal">performing an ISB operation&#13;</span><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:9.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal">    &#8212; </span><sp\an dir="ltr" style="font-size:10.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal">taking an exception&#13;</span><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:9.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal">    &#8212; \</span><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:10.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal">returning from an exception.&#13;    For more information see </span><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:10.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;f\ont-weight:normal;font-style:italic">Generation of debug events</span><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:10.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"> on page C3-40 [PDF page 1644].&#13;    Otherwise, reads and writes to memory\-mapped debug registers have their effects on completion of the read or write operation.</span></p></body>)/RD[0.552429 0.552429 0.552429 0.552429]/Type/Annot/Popup 12545 0 R/AP<</N 18062 0 R>>>>endobj12545 0 obj<</Rect[48.1223 71.5008 567.175 392.227]/Subtype/Popup/Parent 12544 0 R/F 28/Open false/Type/Annot>>endobj12546 0 obj<</Rect[482.477 155.864 502.477 173.864]/CreationDate(D:20101118165056Z)/NM(c11170df-d709-499a-a82b-a11e583f6471)/Subtype/Text/CA 0.505005/C[1.0 1.0 0.0]/F 28/Contents(To clarify the intended meaning of the replacement text:\rThe events listed can be described as context synchronization operations.\r Ä Without a context synchronization operation, the  change becomes visible at some point after the instruction that chang\ed the memory-mapped debug registers. \r Ä However, until a context synchronization operation has occurred, the architecture does not guarantee that the change is visible.\r)/M(D:20110712122058+01'00')/Name/Comment/P 12540 0 R/T(ghawes)/Subj(Sticky Note)/RC(<?xml version="1.0"?><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:8.2.5" xfa:spec="2.0.2" ><p dir="ltr"><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:10.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-we\ight:normal;font-style:normal">To clarify the intended meaning of the replacement text:&#13;The events listed can be described as </span><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:9.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:italic">context syn\chronization operations</span><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:9.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal">.</span><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:10.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal">&#\13;</span><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:9.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"> &#8226; </span><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:10.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:nor